      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{2x-6}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2x-6}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2y-6}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2y-6} \\\\ x(2y-6) & =5 \\\\ 2xy-6x & =5 \\\\ 2xy & =6x+5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{6x+5}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{6x+5}{2x}.\n$$",
